# LINE ENDINGS ARE THE REPOSITORY'S BUSINESS, NOT THE PLATFORM'S.
#
# Without this, `git init` on Windows with the default core.autocrlf=true rewrites every text file
# to CRLF in the working copy, which is what produced eighty "LF will be replaced by CRLF" warnings
# on the first commit here.
#
# It is not only noise. Three separate defects in this family of projects have come from a byte
# that differs between platforms:
#   * `git archive` applies the same conversion as a checkout, so a Windows pack and a Linux pack
#     of ONE commit produced different bytes. deploy_direct.py forces -c core.autocrlf=false for
#     exactly that reason, and tests/test_portability.py asserts it.
#   * a deploy payload sent to `bash -s` in text mode arrived as CRLF and failed with the
#     memorable "$'\r': command not found".
#   * the preview stamp hashed raw bytes, so it silently meant "previewed on this operating
#     system" rather than "this frontend was previewed".
#
# Declaring LF here makes the working copy, the commit and the deployed artifact the same bytes
# everywhere, so none of those can recur through a checkout.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binaries must never be touched. A PNG can legitimately contain the bytes 0d 0a, and "normalising"
# it corrupts the file.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.webp binary
*.ico binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.mp4 binary
*.pdf binary
*.pptx binary
*.xlsx binary
*.docx binary

# Generated, and noisy in a diff. Still committed: the lockfile is what makes the image build
